Abstract

The Blackwell Layered Energy Kernel (BLEK) is a unified mathematical and control‑theoretic framework for modeling layered‑energy systems. These systems appear across physics, climate, biology, infrastructure, economics, and engineered networks. BLEK introduces:

the Blackwell Master Equation,

a tensor formulation for multi‑layer coupling,

a warp‑stack‑style state machine,

a phase diagram for operational regimes,

a collapse cascade model, and

a universal control law for stabilization and steering.

BLEK provides a generalizable architecture for predicting, preventing, and controlling collapse in multi‑layer systems. It is simulation‑ready, control‑ready, and domain‑agnostic.

1. Introduction

Layered‑energy systems are ubiquitous:

atmospheric layers

