BLEK provides such a framework.

It is built on the insight that layered systems share a common structure:energy, curvature, flux, thresholds, and control.

BLEK formalizes this structure into a single kernel.

2. System Definition

A layered‑energy system consists of (N) shells:

[ S_0, S_1, \dots, S_{N-1} ]

Each shell has:

energy (E_i)

curvature/tension (G_i)

flux (J_i)

thresholds for activation, deactivation, and collapse

a discrete state (\sigma_i)

The system evolves according to the Blackwell Master Equation.

3. The Blackwell Master Equation

The core dynamic law is:

[ \frac{dE_i}{dt}

\gamma(G_{i+1}-G_i)

\gamma(G_i-G_{i-1}) + P_i - D_i ]

with curvature defined as:

[ G_i = \beta |E_i - E_{i-1}| ]

This equation governs:

energy flow

curvature response

stability

activation

deactivation

collapse

It is the generator of the entire BLEK architecture.

4. Tensor Formulation

Define:

[ \mathbf{E} = [E_0, E_1, E_2]^T ]

Curvature operator:

[ \boldsymbol{\kappa} = \beta \mathbf{C}\mathbf{E} ]

Curvature gradient:

[ \mathbf{G} = \mathbf{D}\boldsymbol{\kappa} ]

Warp operator:

[ \mathbf{A} = \gamma\beta,\mathbf{L}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C} - \mathbf{D}_{\text{loss}} ]

Tensor master equation:

[ \frac{d\mathbf{E}}{dt}

\mathbf{A}\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{P} ]

This form is simulation‑ready and supports eigenvalue analysis.

5. Stability Analysis

Eigenvalues of (\mathbf{A}):

[ \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 ]

determine system behavior:

Stable: (\Re(\lambda_i)<0)

Breathing/Oscillatory: (\Re(\lambda_i)<0,\ \Im(\lambda_i)\neq 0)

Runaway/Collapse: (\Re(\lambda_i)>0)

This yields the Blackwell Stability Map.

